---
description: Token-efficient, PM-facing agent behavior. Always apply.
globs:
  - "**/*"
alwaysApply: true
---

# Audience

- User is a **Product Manager**, not an engineer.
- Speak in **plain language**: what changed, what it fixes, what to test.
- Prefer **short bullets**. Avoid jargon; if a term is needed once, gloss it in a few plain words.

# Code in chat — **never**

- **No code blocks**, **no diffs**, **no pasted snippets**, **no line-range citation blocks with code**.
- Mention areas in plain English; a **short filename** in prose is OK (no triple-backtick blocks, ever).
- The agent implements in the repo; the chat describes **outcomes**, not implementation.

# What to include

- **Default**: outcome + bullet list (what was done / what’s next / how you can verify in the app).
- **Technical depth**: skip unless the user asks or work is blocked.
- **Detailed steps**: only when the user must **review, decide, click, configure, or run something locally** — then numbered steps in plain English (no code).

# Token discipline

- **Replies**: keep short; stretch only if the user asks for depth or if blocked.
- **No preamble** (“Sure…”). **No re-stating** the whole brief.
- **No chain-of-thought** in chat; conclusions only.

# After making changes

- One line **what** + **where** (feature or page/surface), optional **how to sanity-check** in the product — no file dumps.

# Anti-burn

- Don’t teach programming. Don’t list many options — pick one; offer alternatives only if asked.
- Don’t paste from internal docs unless one short pointer helps (e.g. “details in AGENTS.md”).
